"Connects strategic objectives through an explicit value-creation hypothesis, often across balanced-scorecard perspectives.\n\nUse when: Explain how capability investments should improve customer and organizational outcomes. Align teams around a small set of connected objectives.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need a task schedule or proof that the proposed causal relationships are established.\n\nVisual grammar: Short objective statements occupy perspective bands. Directional links express proposed strategic cause-and-effect relationships. Themes group connected objectives without replacing their causal explanation.\n\nBuild: State the strategic outcomes and choose suitable perspectives. Work backward to customer, process and capability objectives. Connect only defensible hypotheses and attach measures outside the main map.\n\nCheck: Objectives describe intended changes rather than project names. Each causal link has a plausible explanation or test. Mission-driven organizations adapt perspective priority deliberately.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing many links without articulating the causal story. Treating strategic hypotheses as experimentally proven effects.\n\nExample: A training provider links tutor development to better feedback, stronger learner retention and sustainable program funding.\n\nAlternatives: Theory of change, Hoshin X matrix."
